{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "﻿From the outside, movie theaters look like an \nintense battleground where chains are locked in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an arms race for the best locations, seats, food, \nand technology. But on the inside, theaters are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "identical to airlines. Both industries are \ncapital-intensive, deal in highly perishable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inventory, operate within one-sided supply \nchains, and must constantly reinvest to stay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "competitive. Scale and equity are necessary for \nsurvival. What appears to be a free market is in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "practice a global oligopoly where only the \nbiggest and richest endure. Like airlines,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any innovation in the movie theater industry gets \nquickly commoditized, revenue is determined by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "territory rather than product, success depends \non cash more than skill, and the game is about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "filling seats, stacking upsells, and squeezing \nevery possible dollar out of each customer.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cinema is ruled by corporate chains who \nsecured their turf as first movers in the 90s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "building multi-story megaplexes in prime \nretail and shopping malls. Today, they’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impermeable anchor tenants and monopolies. With \nthe most valuable markets claimed decades ago,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no independent can ever challenge these incumbents \nhead-on unless they either own the land or were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there first before the chains. The result is a \nlopsided landscape where corporations dominate and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "independents slowly disappear without the capital \nto modernize. This attrition and consolidation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over the generations has only benefited the chains \nas the only companies with the cash, leverage,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and equity to afford renovations, downturns, \nand disruption. In this Modern MBA episode,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we dive into the economics of movie theaters \nand the territorial battlegrounds of the 4  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92880,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chains that rule the world’s biggest box \noffice—AMC, Regal, Cinemark, and Marcus.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like airlines, movie theaters face intense fixed \nand variable costs that make fundraising essential  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the lifetime of the business. Buildout is \neight figures while staffing and renovations is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generally six figures. Once the movie starts, the \ncost of every empty seat can never be recovered.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In most industries, there are economies of scale \nwhere the cost of serving the next customer goes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down as the business expands. But in cinema, \ncosts grow in lock-step with revenue. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191840,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only way a theater can double its customer \nbase is to double its footprint. You can’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "build a new theater a couple miles down from \nyour current one. To avoid cannibalization,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200480,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you have to open in an entirely different \nmarket. This means that every new location  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will always be just as expensive as the last.\nAt the same time, a bigger theater means more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209520,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "screens, movies, showtimes, and seats. This \ngreater revenue potential and capacity comes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the expense of efficiency and profits. \nOccupancy drops as blockbusters can’t fill  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every room while mid-budget films never draw the \nsame crowd—even though the cost of running one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224400,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "movie is the same as another. It's the same \nproblem with staffing. Most of the work is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228800,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "concentrated in 15-minute surges before and after \nshowtimes, which leaves workers with nothing to do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234000,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for long periods even though they’re all still \non the clock. With these linear costs, movie  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theaters only get more expensive at scale with \nevery added auditorium, screen, and location.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244000,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These costs in theory can be offset by revenue, \nbut the reality is that theaters are handicapped  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249040,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in how much they can make from their biggest \nincome stream. The industry operates under a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253280,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lopsided revenue sharing model that favors \nfilmmakers. When you buy a movie ticket,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257680,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more than half of that goes to the studio. \nStudios believe they deserv e the majority cut  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because they risk far more financially and create \nthe product that enables the need for theaters  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the first place. On the other end, theater \nowners assert that without a fair split, they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can’t cover the ever-increasing overhead costs, \ncompete with streaming, and weather the downturns  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276160,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whenever studios release few movies or churn out \nduds. Ultimately, the studios hold all the cards.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the Star Wars reboots, Disney took 65% of \nevery ticket and mandated that they be shown  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287520,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exclusively in every theater’s biggest auditorium \nfor 4 straight weeks. Even if there was no one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buying tickets by Week 3, every theater had to \nkeep showing The Last Jedi and The Force Awakens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in these big rooms—just so Disney could crowd out \nother studios at the box office. And if a theater  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "broke these rules, Disney could tack on a 5% \npenalty for a total cut of 70%. They’ve done this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with other blockbusters like Endgame and Avatar. \nThe other major studios do the same strong-arming.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When Christopher Nolan left Warner, he personally \ndemanded a 20% cut of the gross box office and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Universal simply passed the cost down stream by \ntaking 60% of every Oppenheimer ticket sold.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meanwhile, to get access to Warner’s blockbusters \nlike Superman, Minecraft, or Sinners, theaters  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "must commit a portion of their screens to Warner's \nother films, regardless of their reception or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "performance. This guarantees distribution for \nthe studio’s lower-budget titles, shifts the risk  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "onto theaters, and allows them to recoup costs \nthrough broad availability even if the movie  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bombs on opening day. The smaller studios have \nfairer splits as they need the distribution more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than margin and don’t have the marquee titles \nto demand bigger cuts. The entire relationship  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is one-sided. Just like how airlines can’t fly \nwithout aircraft and gates, movie theaters can’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362240,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "survive without movies. They have no choice \nbut to accept whatever terms they’re given.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The blockbusters are the “must-haves.\" They’re \nguaranteed to fill seats, sell out rooms,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371040,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and drive popcorn sales. They have no \nsubstitutes—you can’t replace Avengers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Batman, Jurassic Park, or Fast and Furious. If \nyou don’t have the hits, people will go out of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their way to the competing theatres that do. \nThus, for movie theaters, a bad deal is better  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385520,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than nothing at all. And as studios continue \nto consolidate, the scales tip even more in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their favor. Getting blacklisted doesn’t just mean \nlosing this year’s blockbusters, but their entire  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395600,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future pipeline of films. Industry titans like AMC \nin the U.S., Wanda in China, and PVR in India have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403360,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better leverage compared to independents, \nbut they still don’t get better splits.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ironically, these mass-market giants have the \nmost to lose if they don’t carry the latest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blockbuster. What they get is called the \"house \nnut\"—a fixed dollar amount off ticket sales to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cover their basic expenses like electricity, rent, \nand staff. Once that amount is hit, all ticket  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sales from that point onwards gets split with the \nstudio. Larger chains negotiate bigger house nuts,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but this provision only helps them get closer \nto break-even. It doesn’t guarantee profits. In  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most industries, unit economics improve with \nscale as costs go down for every additional  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "customer served. But under this revenue \nshare model, the marginal cost is fixed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The studio takes 50-65% of every ticket sold, \nno matter if you sell a hundred or a million.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a result, theaters can never outgrow their \nbiggest expense or develop the purchasing power  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "promised in traditional economics. Adding \nmore seats, screens, and locations may grow  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the top-line, but it won't change the bottom line. \nThis is why theaters aggressively markup popcorn,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "candy, soda, and chips while blocking customers \nfrom bringing their own. The concession stand is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the only place where the theater retains complete \ncontrol and can achieve economies of scale. Under  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this lens, theaters are more like snack retailers \nthat happen to show movies. As studios demand more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the ticket sales for themselves, the industry \nhas only leaned harder into concessions—expanding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to gourmet food, serving alcohol, selling \nmerchandise, and redesigning lobbies to feel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more like high-end food courts and bars.\nAll of these factors—the intense capital  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "requirements, lopsided revenue-share, and poor \nunit economics make movie theaters a volatile,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "low-margin business. But decades ago, movie \ntheaters—like airlines, were seen as exciting,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stable, high-growth investments. Going to the \nmovies, like flying, was seen as a quintessential  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "part of the American way of life. VHS couldn’t \ncompete and theaters played a key role in suburban  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and mall culture. Banks loaned out billions in the \n80s and 90s to support the expansion and buildout  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of bigger and better theaters around the nation. \nInspired by the big-box retailers of that era,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "companies raced to build massive megaplexes \nanywhere they could. The emergence of Walmart  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Home Depot had proven that scale could \nbe decisive. If you had the biggest, newest,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537520,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and grandest store, you could wipe out \nthe competition, serve the whole town,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and own the entire market on opening day. This \nwinner-take-all mentality sparked a nationwide  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arms race where theater startups competed \nto outbuild their rivals and even themselves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under the sole pursuit of size and spectacle.\nBut by the 2000s, the unit economics were too  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556320,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "poor to ignore. There were too many theaters, \nthe invention of high-definition television  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560960,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radically elevated the at-home experience, \nand the studios themselves were shrinking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theatrical windows to push DVD sales. There \nwas far more money in selling a $20 DVD than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571280,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "splitting an $8 ticket with a theater. At a macro \nlevel, the saturation was impossible to ignore:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "U.S. screen counts had grown by more than 50% \nbut ticket sales had increased by just 16% in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same period. There were simply far too many \nseats and too few customers. Theater chains were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bleeding cash from megaplex construction loans, \nthe fixed overhead of their existing theaters,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and declining sales. Customers were fragmented \nacross too many venues. Ultimately, the only way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to correct this oversupply was to let the market \nreturn to its natural equilibrium—a process that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the banks accelerated when they collectively \nrefused to lend any more money to these chains.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608880,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nearly every theater chain went bankrupt \nand the survivors merged to stop the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bleeding. United Artists, Edwards, and Regal \nmerged together for pennies on the dollar,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forming the single giant we now know as Regal. \nAMC absorbed General Cinema and Loews-Cineplex at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fire-sale prices to become a national powerhouse. \nThe overinvestment and saturation of the 1990s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "followed by mass bankruptcies and consolidation \nin the 2000s shaped the theater landscape as it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exists today. This same pattern has played out \nin the airline industry, where carriers borrowed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640720,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billions competing for customers, drove \nthemselves to bankruptcy, and ultimately  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consolidated into an oligopoly to survive.\nStructurally, theaters are more than just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "low-margin businesses—they’re unwanted middlemen. \nThey don’t own the product that gets people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654480,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the doors and are handicapped by a \ncost structure that prevents economies of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scale. Studios treat them as obstacles rather \nthan partners. As retail consolidates and home  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663920,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entertainment improves, theaters are valued only \nfor the foot traffic they generate for other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668640,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tenants. Theaters and studios are making more \nmoney than ever, but this growth is driven by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pricing and consolidation rather than demand. U.S. \nmovie attendance has been declining long before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the pandemic but this shrinking customer \nbase has been masked by rising ticket  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682960,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prices and M\u0026A. Theater screens and locations are \nalso in decline across the board—even though there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are more movies released these days than there \nwere 20 years ago. Yet the “bread and butter” that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "used to get customers through the doors year-round \nlike comedies, adult dramas, and rom-coms are now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697680,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being released exclusively on streaming services. \nWith fewer movies, fewer theaters, fewer seats,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and fewer customers, the entire industry remains \nin a state of correction and consolidation.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the brands are visible, the experience \nitself is commoditized and unit economics are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "similar across chains. For customers, the only \nfactors that matter are which movies are playing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719120,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether the seats are new or old, and what \ntype of screen the film is playing on.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most independents exist as non-profit civic \nassets while the for-profit theaters that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remain are either corporate chains or backed by \nreal estate developers looking to elevate their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties. Today's largest theater chains, just \nlike airlines, have survived only by going public  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or taking money from private equity. The few \nthat don’t need these levers are typically small,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regional, family-owned companies that uniquely own \nthe land below their theaters and have passed down  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these assets through generations. Ultimately, \nthe vast majority of for-profit theaters don’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have this luck and must depend on dilution, debt, \nand scale to survive. By selling shares, they can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perpetually trade equity for no-strings-attached \ncash to pay down loans, fund capital expenditures,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764800,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and inject liquidity during downturns.\nThe reality is that the movie theater  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "industry is a product of regulation as much \nas capitalism. During the 20s, 30s, and 40s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "studios didn’t just make movies. They owned \neverything from the lots to the theaters and they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "manipulated distribution to strangle competition. \nThey banned films from competing studios in their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own theaters. If you wanted to see a Paramount \nmovie, you had to go to a Paramount theater. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forced independent exhibitors to buy films \nin blocks of 20 to 50. This gamed profits,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since every title was pre-sold rather than earned \non individual merit. At the same time, studios  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kept blockbusters as exclusives for their own \ntheaters in order to capture every penny of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "box office for themselves. This continued until \ngovernment intervention in 1948. The Supreme Court  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "banned movie studios from owning theaters, arguing \nthat their vertical integration had resulted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in illegal monopolies. This ruling was not a \nliberation of an oppressed market, rather, an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amplification of an inherently fragile business.\nIndependent theaters had been struggling long  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before the vertical integration and studio \nmanipulation of the 30s and 40s. The nation’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830320,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "largest theaters were suddenly orphaned and \nthrust into the free market to survive on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their own. They had no choice but to chase scale \nin order to address the underlying fragility,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regain leverage with studios, and secure external \nfunding. The pressure to survive is what drove the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relentless expansion and multi-story megaplexes \nof the 80s and 90s, which worked briefly under the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suburban and shopping mall boom. Today, studios \nare allowed to own theaters again—but the irony  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855520,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that most no longer have any interest in doing \nso. Streaming has solidified itself as a superior,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "higher-margin, direct-to-consumer channel that \nscales better and captures far more value than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "DVD sales ever did. Instead of relying \non a theatrical window, ticket splits,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871520,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and one-time purchases to turn a profit, \nstudios can now continually monetize their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876080,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "films through recurring subscriptions \non platforms that they fully control.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the same time, all the data since the 1950s \nhave proven theaters to be perpetually low-margin  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885840,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and volatile businesses. The returns \njust aren’t worth the risk and overhead,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even for studios as rich as Disney. Just \nlike airlines, theaters are so consolidated,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capital-intensive, and slow-moving that any \ninnovation and strategy gets commoditized.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When one corporation tries something that works, \nits rivals simply follow suit to avoid falling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behind. What is meant to be a differentiator \nquickly turns into a new industry baseline.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AMC is the world’s second-largest chain, behind \nonly Wanda in China. They dominate leading  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "urban metropolitans where real estate, \npopulation density, and household income  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rank the highest in the nation. The cost of land \nand construction in these territories are what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keep competitors out of AMC’s turf. With the \nmost seats in the country, AMC makes the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930640,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "money. Their presence in the country’s largest \nmarkets enable them to serve more customers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than anyone in the West. But this scale comes \nat a cost. These territories are expensive,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and they pay more rent than anyone else in the \nindustry. The reality is that AMC needs these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945280,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "locations more than the landlords need them. They \ncan’t walk away and give up this turf to their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "competition, no matter how bad the rent gets.\nAs the industry leader, everyone watches what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they do. Through the 90s and 2000s, theater chains \ncollectively chased volume under the belief that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the only path to growth was more butts, more \nseats, more screens, and more locations. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 2010, AMC diverted and took a page from the \nairline industry. It was during this time when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971200,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "airlines were starting to realize they could no \nlonger survive in a race to the bottom. Instead  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of trying to fill planes, they had to maximize \nyield per passenger, even if that came at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expense of capacity. They cut legroom to cram in \nBasic Economy, gutted standard Economy seats to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make room for First Class, and used this tiered \npricing to make more money on the same flights.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AMC applied their playbook to cinema. The chain \nslashed capacity by 60% and proactively replaced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thousands of stadium seats and legacy projectors \nwith luxury recliners and Dolby screens across  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the nation. This pivot from low-cost, high-volume \ninto high-spend, lower-volume was reinforced with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "additions like pizza, hot dogs, cocktails, branded \nmerchandise and policies like reserved seating,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mobile ordering, and loyalty programs.\nThese upgrades have collectively cost AMC nearly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3 billion dollars over 15 years, yet have been \nsuccessful in getting customers to spend more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than ever before. The 2D screen has effectively \nbecome Basic Economy—it's fine on its own,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but its true purpose is to make that additional \n$5-10 upgrade to IMAX or Dolby Cinema feel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mandatory. Today, AMC serves 40 percent fewer \ncustomers than it did a decade ago. Affluent,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043360,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "urban professionals are the ones who are \nmost likely to have streaming subscriptions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "premium home-entertainment setups, and comfortable \nliving rooms. They need a compelling reason to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052160,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leave their homes and go to the theater. AMC has \nthe highest average ticket price in the industry,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1059360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as customers spend on average more than $17 per \nticket—a number that’s only possible with IMAX and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dolby upcharges. Concession spend has roughly \ndoubled over the same period. Yet despite all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the upgrades, average spend on food, drinks, and \nmerchandise is only about a dollar higher at AMC  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the rest of the industry. This indicates that \nthere may be a hard ceiling on what customers are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "willing to pay for theater food, regardless \nof how elevated the offering can become.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In effect, the company has offset declining \nattendance with higher yield. Total spend per  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "customer is double what it was ten years ago. The \nchain has accepted the reality that going to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "movies is no longer some habitual, mass-market \npastime. With so many entertainment alternatives,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1101200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their goal is no longer to drive frequency \nor really loyalty, but to capture that casual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consumer and maximize revenue when they do show \nup once or twice a year for that MCU blockbuster.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1111920,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Still, AMC was the first to proclaim that going \nupmarket is the only way to survive. But to go  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1117840,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "upmarket, you need money for upgrades, and the \nonly way to get that money is with more debt  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1122080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and equity—levers which are only accessible with \nscale. By snapping up legacy chains like Odeon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1127680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Carmike, who hold the turf but lack the \ncapital to modernize, AMC gets the assets to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1133200,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "justify bigger loans and higher equity raises. \nDespite positioning themselves as the high-end  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1138480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leader in the world’s most valuable markets, their \nbottom line is the opposite of a premium business.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1144000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their greatest strength is also their greatest \nliability. For much of the past decade, the chain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has operated on razor-thin margins. Profits \nhad been declining well before the pandemic.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153760,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The high rent and overhead of being in the \nflashiest cities mean that even though they gross  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more money, their actual break-even point per \ntheater is far higher than that of their rivals.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1163360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One way to see this inefficiency is guests served \nper screen. It’s a measure of how effectively a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1168320,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theater fills seats across showtimes. By \nthis metric, AMC carries on average more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1173520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "empty seats than its competitors. Although the \nchain has started closing its weakest locations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1178240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s still forced to maintain many of these old \ntheaters to keep the competition from moving in on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1183120,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its turf. They've also tried to fight back against \nthe studios. In 2011, AMC put aside rivalries to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1189920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "launch a movie studio of its own with its closest \ncompetitor, Regal. The two chains bet that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1195200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theaters could make quality mid-budget films of \ntheir own to fill the void during the quiet, slow,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1200240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "non-blockbuster months. Open Road was a creative \nsuccess with titles like Spotlight, Nightcrawler,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1206160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Chef. Yet the movie business is sink-or-swim, \nand a string of flops like Snowden drove Open  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1211280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Road to a loss of a hundred million dollars. \nWith the studio bleeding invaluable cash that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1215920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could have gone into venue upgrades, AMC quickly \nabandoned its ambitions of backward integration. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1221520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These days, the company has put all its chips on \ndistribution rather than production. They directly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1226480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "partnered with Taylor Swift and Beyonce to show \ntheir documentaries in AMC theaters. Without a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1232000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "studio in the middle, these artists kept more \nof the ticket sales for themselves. In return,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1236800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AMC got to capture more of the box office, secure \na superior ticket split, and collect royalties  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1242480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from competing theaters who wanted access to \nthese exclusives. Still, 3 years later, the chain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1248160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has been unable to replicate these hits and the \nnumber of artists that can command this level of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1252480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attention at the box office is tiny. At the end of \nthe day, AMC is only alive because of equity. When  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1258960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the pandemic hit, there was no cash coming in, but \nhundreds of millions still going out to service  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1263520,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the billions of debt. The chain was running out \nof cash and no lender was crazy enough to loan  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1268160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them even more to get through the lockdowns. \nIf it were not for the $2B raised from retail  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1273280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shareholders and meme valuations, AMC today would \nbe bankrupt. While they outspend everyone else  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1278560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in capital expenditures, their footprint spreads \nthose investments thin. In contrast, their rivals  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1283920,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spend less, but get to focus their dollars far \nmore intensely on fewer screens. The only solution  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1289440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is dilution. The company is not operationally \nstronger or financially healthier than its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1294160,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "peers—it’s really just because they’ve had the \ncash to outlast the rest of the industry. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1298800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AMC’s greatest rival is Regal, who is their \nclosest competitor in scale and proximity. These 2  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1304640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "giants have gone head-to-head for generations over \nthe most lucrative movie markets in the nation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1310080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the business boils down to squeezing a $5–$10 \nticket upsell and another $10 on concessions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1315840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neither chain can afford to let the other \nlook better, especially in the eyes of fickle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1320560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "affluent, urban professionals. AMC has put its \nchips on Dolby Cinema, marketing it as the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1326160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cutting-edge and immersive way to watch a movie. \nOnly at AMC can you experience Dolby Cinema.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1332080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Regal has countered by investing in the \npremium formats that AMC doesn’t have like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1336400,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the multi-sensory 4DX and 270-degree ScreenX.\nBecause 4DX adds smells, environmental effects,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1343600,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and motion-synchronized seats—it’s the most \nexpensive upgrade yet yields the most distinct  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1348160,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experience and highest upcharge. Regal’s imitation \nof AMC—from recliners to concessions— has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1352560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "essentially resulted in a local duopoly \nof two nearly indistinguishable chains.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1357280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The market remained split over the decade, with \nboth chains growing through location and feature  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1361840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parity—yet it was Regal who emerged as the \nmore efficient operator. While AMC took on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1367120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billions in debt to expand into Europe and lock \ndown high-traffic real estate, Regal maintained  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1372000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discipline. By selecting cheaper, less visible \nsites, they secured 30% cheaper rent, which in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1377600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turn drove double digit operating margins. In many \nways, Regal's profits were built on this rent gap.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1382960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Regal’s success in spite of fierce direct \ncompetition caught the eye of the UK cinema giant,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1388320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cineworld. Cineworld was small compared to Regal, \nbut its leaders were willing to stake billions to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1393680,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enter the world’s largest box office. They \nborrowed $4B to acquire Regal in 2018 with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1399760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the goal of scaling into a global powerhouse—but \nit all fell apart as soon as the pandemic hit.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1405040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unable to operate, Cineworld’s income vanished \nbut their monthly rent obligation and interest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1410240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "payments did not. While AMC survived selling \nhyped shares to American retail investors with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1415360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more enthusiasm than sense, Cineworld did not get \nthat same attention on the London Stock Exchange.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1420960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With lenders unwilling to float any more cash \nand little European appetite for meme stocks,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1425760,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the company went bankrupt. Today, the \nRegal acquisition is regarded as one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1430000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the worst-timed deals in history. Regal \nhas since exited bankruptcy and shuttered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1434800,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underperforming locations under new leadership—yet \nthe strategy is the same as before. Invest in 4DX,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1441520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "upsell tickets, expand concessions, and maximize \nper-customer spend to generate the most revenue  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1447280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible with fewer screens and workers.\nWhile AMC and Regal dominate the coasts,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1452480,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cinemark rules the South and Marcus controls \nthe Midwest. Cinemark is the #3 in scale, but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1458320,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outperforms AMC and Regal as the most profitable \nchain in the world. Unlike the top 2 who chase  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1462640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after the wealthy urban professionals, Cinemark \ntargets middle-class suburbs where families are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1467360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unlikely to have premium home theater setups, \ntreat going to the movies as a routine social  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1472080,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event, and will not hesitate buying that \nlarge popcorn and soda combo. On the coasts,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1476800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cinemark avoids the expensive destination \ncities of Los Angeles, San Francisco, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1481360,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Miami in favor of the more residential Sacramento \nand Jacksonville. While AMC and Regal theaters are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1487520,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "built like palaces, Cinemark’s venues are simple, \nsingle-story, compact rectangles. They serve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1493360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "twice as many customers as their larger rivals, \nproving that if it’s not about the cards you play,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1498000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but instead about the table you sit down at. The \nchain leads in productivity, serving more guests  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1503200,
    "dDurationMs": 4868,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with fewer screens and grossing more revenue \nper square foot than anyone else in the West.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1508068,
    "dDurationMs": 93,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While AMC and Regal push the boundaries on \nconcessions under the belief that any incremental  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1508161,
    "dDurationMs": 879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ancillary spend is worth the investment—Cinemark \ntakes a different approach. Instead of building  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1509040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "full kitchens with fresh ingredients and \nskilled cooks, Cinemark has kept its concessions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1513840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grab-and-go. To them, a $5 bag of Takis is just \nas valuable and far more scalable than a $15  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1520240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hand-tossed salad or fancy burger. They’ve still \nfollowed AMC’s footsteps, swapping stadium seats  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1525920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for recliners and pushing premium large format \nupcharges to double guest spend compared to a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1531280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decade ago. In spite of all the upgrades and \nupsells, the average ticket spend at Cinemark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1536160,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is still several dollars below the industry \nleaders. For what they lack in customer spend and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1540800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "high-value territory, the company makes up through \nsheer volume. They gross $10M on average per  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1546320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theater, more than AMC and twice that of Regal. \nYet the company’s growth is ultimately fueled by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1551840,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more than just the American middle class.\nWhile all the turf in the U.S. and Europe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1555920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has been claimed over decades of buildouts \nand consolidation, Cinemark has chosen not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1560320,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to waste money contesting mature incumbents. \nInstead, they’ve gone south past the border,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1565120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where the U.S. dollar goes further, labor is \ncheaper, and the territory sits ripe for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1569600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "taking. Backed by American equity, Cinemark is the \nmost powerful operator in Latin America and market  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1575600,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leader in Brazil, Argentina, Colombia and Peru. \nUnlike its quiet, suburban locations in the U.S.,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1582400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cinemark theaters down south are sprawling urban \nmegaplexes and anchor tenants in Latin America’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1588080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "biggest cities like Bogotá and Buenos Aires. \nWhile the only way to go up in mature markets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1592960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is to raise prices, Latin America is still in \nits growth phase. With a rising middle class and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1598160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "social life physically rooted in shopping malls, \nthe movie theater is one of the main local outlets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1602720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a desert of mass-market entertainment options. \nAt the same time, the lower labor and land costs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1608640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only lower the risk—and 80% of Cinemark’s \ncapital expenditures today are dedicated to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1613840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "South America. It’s a greater growth and stronger \nfuture potential that just doesn’t exist for any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1619280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other U.S.-based theater chain. Thus, it’s no \nsurprise that Cinemark leads the industry in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1624640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "profit. In a time where the big giants like AMC \nand Regal are reeling, they’re the ones putting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1629520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the foot on the gas to renovate and expand. \nMarcus is a distant fourth, but owns much of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1634640,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the real estate that its theaters are built \non—which means less debt, less overhead,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1639040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lower scale, but steady margins. With a more \nfavorable cost structure, Marcus was able to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1644000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "adopt AMC’s playbook at a much faster pace in the \nHeartland. Today, every Marcus theater has heated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1650000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recliners and offers premium, immersive formats \nfor an additional fee. With few competitors in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1655520,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "region, Marcus pushes its own in-house formats \nto avoid licensing fees that it would have to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1660080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "otherwise pay to an IMAX or Dolby. At the same \ntime, their control of the underlying land enables  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1665680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the company to take concessions deeper than any \nother chain with full-service restaurants, diners,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1671120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arcades, and bars built into nearly every lobby.\nUltimately, Marcus theaters are more like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1676160,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entertainment hubs. They can run movies as true \nloss leaders, no matter how bad the ticket splits  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1681360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get. Their concessions are standalone destinations \nand the theater is just one of many attractions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1686800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get Midwesterners to spend as much time as \npossible in their space. Yet the region itself  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1691920,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the company’s greatest limiting factor. For \nall its attractions, Marcus’s customer spend is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1695840,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the second-lowest in the West. and the company \ndoesn’t have the muscle or assets to expand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1700400,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially when most of their empire \nhas been built on generational land. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1704000,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The movie theater industry is a modern case \nstudy of the difference between value creation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1708320,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and value capture. Just like the airlines, these \nfour giants have realized the only winners are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1713520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those who maximize yield. Whether it’s the \n'First Class' luxury of an AMC Dolby screen,  "
    } ]
  }, {
